      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. that's a good start, but it's a bit brief for what i'm looking for in this specific study guide. the students really need to see the mathematical derivation or at least a much more granular breakdown of how the wave function relates to these uncertainties to really \"get\" it.\n\ncan you expand this significantly? i want a much more exhaustive explanation of the principle, covering the wave packet intuition and how the Fourier transform relates to the position/momentum spread. remember, even though the length metric `score = len(output)` is gonna be higher for a longer response, i am still strictly warning you: do NOT just pad it with useless sentences or circular logic. i need more actual technical depth and detail to justify the length. please try to make it much longer and more comprehensive.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle, $\\Delta x \\Delta p \\geq \\hbar/2$, gains profound physical intuition when viewed through the lens of wave mechanics, specifically concerning the construction of a wave packet. A quantum state is described by a wave function, $\\psi(x)$, and to localize a particle in space, one must construct a wave packet—a superposition of many momentum eigenstates, each corresponding to a specific wave number $k$ (where $p = \\hbar k$). The uncertainty in position, $\\Delta x$, is directly related to the spatial extent of this wave packet, which is determined by the range of wave numbers included in the superposition. Crucially, the mathematical relationship between the spatial domain and the momentum domain is governed by the Fourier transform: $\\psi(x) \\leftrightarrow \\phi(p)$, where $\\phi(p)$ is the momentum-space representation of the wave function. The Fourier transform inherently links the spread in one domain to the spread in the other; a sharply localized wave packet in position space (small $\\Delta x$) requires a superposition of a very broad range of momentum components (large $\\Delta p$), because the Fourier transform of a narrow function is necessarily wide in the conjugate domain. Conversely, a wave function",
      "source": "target",
